<p>Hardware goes out on the lorry or the pick-up. HardPoint prints the delivery note, tracks it from the yard to the site, and charges the
  customer for transport, either at the till or afterwards. Deliveries are switched on under <strong>Settings › Features</strong>.</p>

<h2>Charging transport at the till</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Ring up the goods as usual.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Add transport</strong>, type the charge and where it's going (the customer's address is filled in if they have one),
    and press <strong>Add to sale</strong>.</li>
  <li>Transport appears as its own line on the sale and the receipt, with VAT like any other line, and is paid with the rest of the sale.</li>
</ol>
<p>Transport lines keep the amount you typed: customer price lists and quantity breaks don't change them.</p>

<h2>Making the delivery note</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open the sale and press <strong>Deliver</strong>.</li>
  <li>Check the delivery address, and add the transport charge if it wasn't on the sale (see below).</li>
  <li>Print the delivery note. It has a line for the customer to sign.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Charging transport afterwards</h2>
<p>If the customer asks for delivery after paying, type the <strong>Transport charge</strong> on the delivery note. HardPoint makes an
  order for the customer for that amount, which they pay at the till (it's loaded like any other order) or which goes on their account.
  The delivery note links to the order and shows whether it's paid.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A transport charge afterwards needs a named customer. For a walk-in customer, add transport at the till before they pay.</li>
  <li>If transport is already on the sale, the delivery note says so, so it isn't charged twice.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Out and delivered</h2>
<ol>
  <li>When the lorry leaves, mark the note <strong>dispatched</strong> with the driver and vehicle.</li>
  <li>When it arrives, mark it <strong>delivered</strong> with who received it and a photo of the signed note.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Sell › Deliveries</strong> lists what's due out, and Home shows deliveries due today. Send the customer the delivery note on
  WhatsApp so they know what's coming: see <%= link_to "Sending documents on WhatsApp", help_article_path("whatsapp"), class: "link" %>.</p>
